  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed scheme would represent a cramped form of back garden development outside an API that fails to respect the established pattern of residential development in the area. Furthermore, the proposal fails to preserve or make a positive contribution to the open, verdant character and visual quality and appearance of both the site itself and its wider setting. As such, the proposal is unacceptable in principle and contrary to Policy H1 of the London Plan 2021, Policy 1, 7, 13 and 28 of the Sutton Local Plan 2018 and advice provided within Sutton's Supplementary Planning Document 14 Urban Design Guide 'Creating Locally Distinctive Places' (SPD14).",
    "The proposed development, due to its excessive scale of built form within the rear residential garden setting comprising a modest timber shed and a significant mature tree, would appear as an overly dominant and disproportionate structure, adversely impacting on the host property, the streetscene in this stretch of Nicholas Road and the junction with Claydon Drive, Oakley Avenue and the wider area. The proposal fails to respond to the characteristics of the site and as a result, the development would harm the open and verdant character of this location that provides attractive visual appeal from multiple vantage points. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policy 28 of the Sutton Local Plan 2018 and Sutton's Supplementary Planning Document 14 - Urban Design Guide 'Creating Locally Distinctive Places' (SPD14).",
    "The proposed development, by reason of its excessive size, height, depth and proximity of the proposed dwelling to the shared boundary with 21 Oakley Avenue and the donor property, and excessive height and depth in proximity to the rear gardens of odd numbered properties fronting Oakley Avenue to the east of the application site, and No. 1 Nicholas Road to the south, would result in a harmful impact on the outlook from the gardens and rear windows to the detriment of the neighbouring occupiers, creating a sense of enclosure. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policy 29 of the Sutton Local Plan 2018.",
    "The proposed development, due to its excessive size, height and depth close to the shared boundaries, and location to the south of the donor house's amenity area and south-west of No. 21 Oakley Avenue's rear garden, would result in an unacceptable loss of light to the adjoining occupants at both No. 21 Oakley Avenue and the donor property, contrary to Policy 29 of the Sutton Local Plan 2018.",
    "The felling of mature tree T1 (Douglas Fir), which is considered to be of a high amenity value due to its substantial contribution to the public visual amenity and the broader landscape character of the area, would result in an adverse impact on a significant mature tree within the site and a failure to protect the site's natural features. Furthermore, given the constrained nature of the site, there is insufficient room for a replacement tree of suitable scale to mature and adequately mitigate the loss of tree T1. As such, the proposal is contrary to the aims of Policy G7 of the London Plan 2021 and Policy 28 of the Sutton Local Plan 2018.",
    "Without evidence to the contrary by way of sufficiently detailed SuDS Report and water run-off calculations (Part G calculations), the application has failed to demonstrate that the proposal will provide satisfactory run-off measures and water efficiency calculations. As such, the application would be contrary to Policy SI 13 of the London Plan 2021 and Policies 32 and 33 of the Sutton Local Plan 2018."
  ],
